Elite Performance Lab Dashboard (Streamlit + Interactive Analytics UI)
1. Data Provenance and Methodology
The Elite Performance Lab Dashboard is built on a relational athlete performance dataset covering 15 elite athletes across five sports: Tennis, Basketball, Formula 1, Soccer, and Breaking. The dataset is designed as a synthetic but structured representation of athlete physiology, nutrition, and training behavior, enabling cross-sport comparison under a unified schema.
Rather than relying on live instrumentation or continuous biometric tracking, the dataset integrates publicly available athlete profiles, sports journalism insights, sports science literature, and representative nutrition and training assumptions. These sources are harmonized into a consistent analytical model to allow comparability across fundamentally different athletic disciplines.
The dataset is organized into three primary relational layers:
Body Composition Data
Athlete physical attributes—including body weight, height, and estimated body fat percentage—are compiled from publicly available athlete information and sports reference materials. These variables form the foundation for derived metrics such as BMI, lean mass, fat mass, and weight-normalized performance indicators.
Nutritional Data
Nutrition inputs are constructed from representative meal logs mapped to macronutrient composition (protein, carbohydrates, and fats). These are aggregated at the athlete level to compute total energy intake, macro distributions, and per-kilogram intake ratios. This enables comparison of fueling strategies across sports with different metabolic demands.
Training Session Data
Training workload is represented through session-level records including duration (minutes), intensity classification (high, moderate, low), and derived energy-related proxies such as calories per minute and calories per kilogram. These variables are used to analyze training load structure and its relationship to fueling patterns.
2. Visualization Architecture and Design Motivation
The dashboard is implemented as a Streamlit-based interactive analytics application, using Plotly for all visualizations. The design emphasizes fast exploration of athlete-level and sport-level patterns through linked filtering, hover interactions, and metric switching.
Body Composition Intelligence (Scatter + Pie + Bar Views)
The core body composition analysis uses a multi-variable scatter plot:
X-axis: Body weight (kg)
Y-axis: Body fat percentage (%)
Bubble size: Height (cm)
Color: Sport category
This visualization enables simultaneous comparison of size, composition, and sport-specific clustering. Hover tooltips expose derived metrics such as lean mass, BMI, and nutrition variables.
Additional supporting views include:
Sport distribution pie chart
Sport-level average comparison bar chart with selectable metrics (e.g., calories, protein, session duration, body fat)
Together, these views allow users to identify structural differences between endurance, power, and precision-based sports.
Macronutrient Analysis (Stacked Bars + Normalized Heatmap)
Nutritional patterns are visualized through multiple complementary views:
A stacked bar chart shows macronutrient energy distribution (protein, carbohydrates, fat) across athletes, expressed as percentage of total caloric intake. This highlights differences in dietary structure across sports.
A secondary analysis normalizes nutrient intake per kilogram of body weight, enabling fair comparison across athletes of different sizes. This is visualized using a heatmap that highlights relative density patterns across protein, carbohydrate, fat, and caloric intake.
These views emphasize both absolute intake and body-size-adjusted nutritional strategies.
Training Load and Fueling Analysis (Scatter + Bar + Aggregations)
Training behavior is analyzed through multiple linked plots:
Scatter plots compare session duration with total calorie intake to evaluate alignment between training load and fueling behavior.
Bar charts show calories per minute of training, revealing relative fueling efficiency across athletes.
Additional views normalize intake per kilogram of body weight to assess relative energy demand.
At the sport level, aggregated metrics are used to compare:average session duration; average calorie intake rate; average protein intake per kilogram
This allows identification of structural differences in training intensity and fueling strategies across sports categories.
3. Key Findings
Analysis of the dataset reveals distinct sport-specific physiological and nutritional patterns. First, body composition clusters strongly by sport. Formula 1 drivers and Breaking athletes occupy the lowest body weight and body fat ranges, reflecting performance requirements that prioritize agility and power-to-weight efficiency. In contrast, basketball players show the highest body mass and height distributions while maintaining relatively low body fat, reflecting reliance on lean mass rather than adiposity.
Second, nutritional analysis shows substantial variation in macronutrient distribution across athletes. Carbohydrate intake dominates in endurance- and reaction-based sports such as Tennis and Formula 1, while protein intake varies widely but remains generally below commonly cited strength-training benchmarks when normalized by body weight.
Third, training load and fueling relationships are not fully aligned. Some athletes show relatively low caloric intake relative to session duration, suggesting potential under-fueling compared to training volume. When normalized metrics such as calories per minute and calories per kilogram are examined, differences in fueling efficiency across sports become more apparent.
Finally, the interactive design significantly improves interpretability. The combination of sport-level filtering, metric switching, and multi-view comparisons enables faster identification of structural patterns than static reporting formats.
4. Future Outlook
The current dashboard functions as an exploratory analytics system built on structured but static data. Its next evolution would involve scaling toward more automated and dynamic data pipelines.
Future enhancements could include integration with larger athlete datasets, real-world wearable data (where available), and more granular session tracking. This would allow more accurate modeling of training load, recovery patterns, and nutrition timing effects.
On the modeling side, derived features such as energy balance, rolling workload averages, and sport-specific normalization curves could support deeper performance benchmarking. From a product perspective, the system could evolve into a decision-support tool for coaches and sports scientists, enabling standardized comparison across athletes and supporting more data-driven nutrition and training decisions.
